---
/**
 * The site's 404 page (#1887).
 *
 * Added here because the document layer's legacy-path redirect depends on it, and because its
 * absence was a real bug on its own. Cloudflare Pages, with no `404.html` in the build, falls
 * back to serving the ROOT `index.html` with status **200** for every unmatched path — so until
 * now a mistyped or dead URL returned the network directory page and told the browser, search
 * engines, and any link checker that it was the page you asked for.
 *
 * For `functions/network/[site]/site/documents/[[rel]].ts` that behaviour is fatal rather than
 * merely untidy: it decides whether a path is a retired document URL by asking the asset server
 * first (`ctx.next()`) and redirecting only what genuinely 404s. With no 404 page, nothing ever
 * 404s, and every cited legacy document URL would quietly serve the directory instead of
 * redirecting to the document.
 */
import Base from "~/layouts/Base.astro";
import { withBase } from "@watermark/core/base";
---

<Base
  title="Not found"
  section="directory"
  description="That page isn't here."
  noindex
>
  <article class="prose">
    <h1>Not found</h1>
    <p class="section-lead">
      There's nothing at this address. If you followed a link from somewhere else, it may point at
      a page that has since moved.
    </p>
    <p>
      Source documents were re-addressed in 2026: each one now lives at a short permanent handle
      rather than a path mirroring its folder in the corpus. Old document links redirect
      themselves, so if you reached this page from one, the document has most likely been
      withdrawn from the catalog rather than moved.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><a href={withBase("/")}>The network directory</a> — every watershed point</li>
      <li><a href={withBase("/search")}>Search</a></li>
      <li><a href={withBase("/about")}>About this project</a></li>
    </ul>
  </article>
</Base>
